<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Laois</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Queen's County</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County Laois is an inland county in the Irish midlands, in the province of Leinster. It takes its name from the Loígis, whose kingdom was confiscated under Mary I and shired in 1556 as Queen's County, part of the first English plantation in Ireland together with neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-offaly">King's County</a>. Its county town is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portlaoise">Portlaoise</a>, formerly Maryborough; the name Laois was adopted after Irish independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Laois is the 23rd of Ireland's 32 counties in both area and population. It is landlocked and, uniquely, doubly landlocked, since none of the counties that border it touches the coast. The county was formerly divided into nine baronies, among them Ballyadams, Cullenagh, Portnehinch, Slievemargy, Stradbally and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-ossory">Upper Ossory</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first people in Laois were hunter-gatherers who passed through about 8,500 years ago. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> farmers left burial mounds at Clonaslee and Cuffsborough, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> people left a stone circle at Monamonry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> at Clopook and Monelly. The body of Cashel Man indicates that ritual killing took place around 2000 BCE. The county takes its name from Loígis, a medieval kingdom of which it is only a part. By the first century CE the western third of Laois belonged to the Kingdom of Ossory, while the east was divided among the Seven Septs of Loígis, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omore">O'More</a>, O'Lalor, O'Doran, O'Dowling and O'Devoy.</p><p>Holy men and women founded religious communities in Loígis. St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciaran-of-saighir">Ciarán of Saighir</a> founded a monastery in the western Slieve Bloom Mountains as first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Ossory. Between 550 and 600 St Canice founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghaboe">Aghaboe Abbey</a>, and St Mochua a community at Timahoe. The Synod of Rathbreasail, which established the Irish dioceses, met near Mountrath in 1111. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-of-dunamase">Rock of Dunamase</a> was part of the dowry of the Irish princess Aoife, who married the Norman Strongbow in 1170. From 1175 until about 1325 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> held the best land while Gaelic society withdrew to the bogs, forests and mountains, and in the early 14th century a Gaelic revival drove them out.</p><p>In 1548 the English confiscated the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omore">O'Mores</a> and built the Fort of Leix, today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portlaoise">Portlaoise</a>. In 1556 Queen Mary made the area a county, Queen's County. Two plantations followed, in 1556 and 1607. The first, led by Thomas Radclyffe, 3rd Earl of Sussex, who sought to dispossess the O'Moore clan, provoked a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war and is widely regarded by historians as a failure; the second, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, was more successful. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> settled at Mountmellick in 1659 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> at Portarlington in 1696. The Great Famine of 1845 to 1849 devastated the county, and Irish was still spoken there in the 1870s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The county was known as Queen's County from 1556 to 1922 and received its present Irish name after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-war-of-independence">Irish War of Independence</a>. Portlaoise, formerly Maryborough, is its main town and the seat of Laois County Council. The county had 91,657 people at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Houses built by Anglo-Irish landowners include Durrow Castle, Heywood House and Emo Court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “County Laois” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Loígis were a people of the Irish midlands in what is now <b>County Laois</b>, which takes its name from them.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-loigis">Kingdom of Loigis</a></li></ul></section>
